# SimpleOS aarch64 hello-world in-guest execution entry (EDK2/AAVMF real-firmware lane).
#
# Boot chain: EDK2/AAVMF pflash -> vendor/limine/BOOTAA64.EFI (a real EFI
# application on a FAT ESP) -> this kernel ELF, loaded with `protocol: limine`.
# NEVER QEMU `-kernel`, NEVER isa-debug-exit — the same artifact must be able to
# boot on hardware (.claude/rules/board-runnable.md). The gate that runs this
# (scripts/check/check-simpleos-aarch64-hello-world-in-guest-efi.shs) self-checks
# the assembled QEMU argv for both forbidden flags before starting the VM.
#
# What it proves: a hello-world program WRITTEN IN SIMPLE and compiled to a
# freestanding aarch64 ELF by the RUST SEED (the pure-Simple self-hosted
# compiler cannot compile anything yet — two located bugs) runs inside the guest
# under real firmware and prints ITS OWN output to the PL011 console.
#
# HONEST SCOPE — read this before citing the gate:
# The program runs in KERNEL mode (EL1), not in a ring-3/EL0 user task loaded off
# the guest's own filesystem. The x86_64 sibling lane reaches ring 3 because
# os.kernel.memory.vmm_address_space + os.kernel.arch_adapt.x86_64_user_entry
# provide an x86_64-only map+enter path; there is no aarch64 counterpart in
# src/os today (aarch64 has no `_aarch64_enter_user`, and the Limine aarch64 boot
# lane never builds a user address space). Closing that is a separate, larger
# piece of work and is filed rather than papered over. What IS proven here is
# strictly: real firmware -> real EFI application -> Simple-compiled guest code
# executing and emitting its own marker on serial.
#
# Freestanding discipline (learned from limine_entry.spl and the x86_64 lane):
#   * The entry function MUST be named literally `_start` — native-build only
#     auto-generates the `--defsym=_start=<mangled>` alias for a top-level
#     function of that exact name in the entry file. `ENTRY(kernel_main)` in
#     boot/linker_limine.ld would otherwise resolve to nothing, the entry point
#     would be 0x0, and --gc-sections would discard the whole reachable graph,
#     yielding a "successful" ~1KB link with no .text.
#   * This file lives in examples/09_embedded/simple_os/arch/aarch64/ so that
#     boot-object autodiscovery finds the sibling boot/freestanding_runtime.c
#     (it keys off the --entry file's own directory).
#   * No module-level `val` initializers; constants stay function-local.

use os.kernel.log.klog_api.{log_init_serial, log_raw_println, LOG_INFO}

@unsafe(reason: "bare WFE park has no artifact-verified contract", capabilities: [ffi])
extern fn rt_aarch64_wfe_spin()

fn hello_world_program() -> i64:
    """The hello-world program itself.

    Deliberately a separate function from the boot glue so its output is
    attributable to the PROGRAM and not to a boot stage: every line it emits
    carries the HELLO_ prefix, and the gate's must-FAIL fixture is a serial
    capture containing only the boot lines with these stripped out.

    Returns 0 on success, which the entry reports as the program's exit code.
    """
    log_raw_println("HELLO_NATIVE_SIMPLEOS_AARCH64_OK hello world from Simple")
    log_raw_println("HELLO_NATIVE_SIMPLEOS_AARCH64 second line proves the program kept running")
    0

@noreturn
fn _start():
    """aarch64 hello-world ELF entry point.

    Limine hands off at EL1 with the MMU on, a valid stack, and no arguments —
    no assembly boot glue is needed here (unlike riscv64/x86_64, whose `_start`
    must set up their own stack/mode first).
    """
    log_init_serial(LOG_INFO)
    log_raw_println("[hello] SimpleOS aarch64 hello-world in-guest (EDK2/AAVMF -> BOOTAA64.EFI)")
    log_raw_println("[hello] serial up, invoking the Simple hello-world program")
    val rc = hello_world_program()
    log_raw_println("[hello] native program exited rc={rc}")
    log_raw_println("[hello] parking")
    while true:
        unsafe:
            rt_aarch64_wfe_spin()
